  "example": "A requirements input selects `requirements.cli.*`. A selected requirement references `glossary.command`, which is captured as a separate definition input despite not matching that selection. An unreferenced definition named `requirements.cli.unrelated` is not selected merely because its name matches the selection. {cite}`requirements.definitions.selection`",
  "determination": false,
  "rationale": "The example has one `{test}` reference, `globs_exclude_terms_but_capture_referenced_terms_outside_the_glob` in tests/definitions.rs. It covers most of the scenario but does not assert one of the three outcomes the example states.\n\n**What the test covers**\n- The setup mirrors the example. The glob is `rules.*`, and `rules.ready` references `glossary.a`. That term references `glossary.b`, so both fall outside the glob. A definition `rules.unrelated` is added to `md/rules.md`, and nothing references it.\n- `inputs[0].matches == [\"rules.ready\"]` shows the glob selects only requirements and examples. The matching-name `rules.unrelated` definition is not selected.\n- `inputs[1].definition == \"glossary.a\"` and `inputs[2].definition == \"glossary.b\"` show that referenced definitions outside the glob are captured as separate definition inputs. This includes the transitive one.\n\n**The gap**\n- The example also says the unreferenced `rules.unrelated` is not captured as a definition input just because its name matches the glob. The test does not check this directly.\n- The record's `inputs` length is never asserted. If a buggy implementation appended a `definition: rules.unrelated` input, it would sort after `glossary.*` as `inputs[3]`. The test would still pass.\n- The final check, `!status.contains(\"rules.unrelated\")`, proves nothing about dependencies. `status_markdown_with_options` in src/commands.rs skips every `DefinitionKind::Term`, so `rules.unrelated` would never appear in status output whether or not it was captured.\n- The other tests that assert `inputs.len() == 3` do not include an unreferenced `rules.*` definition, so they don't cover this case either.\n\nThe test needs an explicit assertion. For example, it could check that the inputs length is 3, or that no input has `definition == \"rules.unrelated\"`. No FIXME acknowledges this gap, so the judgment is false.",
